WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:23.200
The ability to move through the air in any direction you wish, to cross continents and

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:29.760
oceans, to range over forests and deserts and mountains, all this birds have been able

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:40.880
to do for 150 million years, but they weren't the first or indeed the last in the skies.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:47.400
We are setting out to explore one of the most astonishing stories in the natural world.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:52.960
The way in which animals manage to rise up from the surface of the earth and colonise

00:00:52.960 --> 00:01:04.960
the air, from the dazzling aerobatics of the insects to the majesty of ancient winged

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:23.840
reptiles, the splendour and agility of birds and the sonar guided precision of night flying

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:29.440
bats.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:42.280
Night has been the key to the success of some of our planet's most remarkable inhabitants.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:51.200
To analyse their spectacular skills we will use the latest technology and we will travel

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:59.880
around the world, from the jungles of Borneo to the fossil filled rocks of China and the

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:04.280
cloud forests of Ecuador.

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:32.840
We will take you into the air and travel with animals as they fly.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:47.920
Evidence for the very beginnings of this astonishing story can be found close to home in the fens

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.920
of Cambridge.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:59.360
Here live creatures that have an ancestry stretching back millions of years.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:05.560
Nobody knows exactly how the first flying animals in the world evolved but there are

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:12.000
creatures alive today that can take us back to those far distant, remarkable times and

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:21.200
they live surprisingly under water.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:28.240
Looking down through the surface to the river bed is like travelling back in time over 320

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:32.080
million years.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:38.440
It was then in an age long before even the dinosaurs evolved that creatures like this

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:45.520
first appeared in the waters of the earth.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:04:05.120
This is an insect, a ferocious predator with jaws like a mechanical grab.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:16.440
It seems unlikely that this animal's ancestors were among the first creatures ever to fly.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:24.720
But this one is not yet adult, it's a larva and it doesn't spend all its life in the water.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:31.960
It has another life and another body above the surface.

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:38.800
Only one morning it climbs up a reed.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:48.120
A split appears in its skin and a very different looking creature begins to emerge.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:54.720
It has four lumps on its back that might perhaps ancestrally have become either gills or protective

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:56.760
armour plates.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:04.760
But now they develop into something very different.

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:10.760
Wings.

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:14.760
It has two pairs of them.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:22.240
Liquid from its body is pumped down along veins to stretch them tight.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:34.440
As they dry in the sun, they harden.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:41.680
The water living dragon has become the dragonfly and the four winged apparatus that it uses

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:51.080
to get into the air is the earliest that we know.

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:55.360
Imprints of such wings have been found in rocks that were laid down on the bottom of

00:05:55.360 --> 00:06:00.560
ancient lakes and streams.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:06.320
This specimen is about 150 million years old.

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:16.800
And at nearly 300 million years, this is the oldest complete wing yet discovered.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:23.720
Ancient and modern wings share a structure that is strikingly similar.

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:29.920
So today's dragonflies are amazingly living fossils that can show us how the very first

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:48.920
flyers overcame the pull of gravity and took to the skies.

00:06:48.920 --> 00:07:06.520
Their wings are marvels of natural engineering.

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:13.640
But to see how they lift a dragonfly into the air, we need to slow the action down.

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:16.440
In principle, it looks fairly simple.

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:22.880
Each wing beats down, pushing on the air below, so lifting the dragonfly up.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:28.520
But each beat also creates another air current that lifts the dragonfly in a very different

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:30.400
way.

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:35.560
And I can demonstrate it using this strip of paper to represent a wing.

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:49.800
If I blow across the top of it, it'll rise much.

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:54.280
That is because the faster air moves, the lower its pressure.

00:07:54.280 --> 00:08:00.520
So I created a lower pressure above the wing, and in consequence, it was sucked upwards.

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:14.400
The problem for a flying animal is to recreate that difference in airspeed.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:24.280
The way the dragonfly does this is remarkable.

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:32.240
As it moves through the air, we can see that it twists its wings at different angles.

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:38.240
On the powerful downbeat, it holds them at a slight upwards angle to the airflow.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:42.900
And this produces an extraordinary effect above the wing.

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:48.840
It creates a swirl behind the leading edge, which spins the air around, increasing the

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:56.160
speed of the air current over the top of the wing, and just a tiny increase in speed generates

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:06.120
a significant upwards force, lifting up the wing and the dragonfly.

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:11.880
The dragonfly can then change the direction of its wingbeats to propel it forwards as

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:19.720
well as upwards.

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:27.360
Remarkably, a dragonfly can beat each of its four wings independently.

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:34.400
And that enables it to perform an astonishing variety of maneuvers.

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:39.680
It can hover.

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:43.240
It can glide.

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:47.400
It can even fly backwards.

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:59.760
For maximum power, it beats both pairs together and can make really sharp turns.

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:09.120
So the very first dragonflies were able to extend their territories far and wide.

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:16.400
And as more insects joined them in the skies, the dragonflies had the skills to be deadly

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:32.160
aerial hunters.

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:36.480
The ability to fly brought great advantages to those early insects.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:42.720
It enabled them to find food, to escape from predators, and particularly important, to

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:50.480
travel to new territories in search of a mate.

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:56.680
Glamzleflies, like their close relations dragonflies, have remained virtually unchanged for millions

00:10:56.680 --> 00:10:59.440
of years.

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:03.520
Mating can be quite complicated when both partners can fly.

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:10.440
And these were among the first kind of animals that had to deal with that problem.

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:15.440
The blue color of this one shows that it's a male.

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:24.040
To attract a female, a male must have something to offer her, a territory.

00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:31.680
He chooses a stretch of water that is likely to contain plenty of food for his offspring.

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:37.360
And he guards this territory against any rivals.

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:43.020
Until a female flies in and joins him.

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:50.560
He must now grab her before she changes her mind, in midair if necessary.

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:56.360
He uses claspers at the tip of his abdomen to grip her behind her neck.

00:11:56.360 --> 00:12:03.280
Amazingly, the pair are able to coordinate the beats of their eight wings.

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:09.680
They may mate in the air or choose a secluded perch where they'll be safe from predators.

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:26.560
They then fly around the territory, laying their fertilized eggs.

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:33.160
Flight enabled insects to invade part of the planet that until then had been uninhabited,

00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:34.160
the air.

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:37.160
And they flourished.

00:12:37.160 --> 00:12:46.760
So, 320 million years ago, the skies thronged with flying insects.

00:12:46.760 --> 00:12:53.720
But those early four-winged forms were destined to produce a whole range of spectacular, highly

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:59.000
specialized fliers.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:08.160
The need to lay eggs in water tied the first dragonflies to streams and ponds like these.

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:14.740
But then, around 20 million years after their arrival, a new kind of flying insect appeared

00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:17.800
with no such ties to water.

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:41.440
To find spectacular proof of their success, we're headed next to Borneo.

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:46.680
To find the next chapter in our evolutionary story of the flying insects, I've come to

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:50.000
Borneo.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:53.400
The very first fliers had two pairs of wings.

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:57.520
Now we're looking for their successes.

00:13:57.520 --> 00:14:04.400
One group of creatures adapted that original four-winged design with such success that

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:10.120
they diversified into the most numerous and widespread group of animals on the entire

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:11.120
planet.

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:37.080
You can find some of the most spectacular examples down there in the rainforest.

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:39.880
Not all insects are hunters.

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:44.280
Some are strict vegetarians like this one.

00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:50.920
This is the landliving equivalent to that underwater monster, the dragonfly larva.

00:14:50.920 --> 00:14:57.800
But this larva, instead of catching little fish and water fleas, munches wood pulp.

00:14:57.800 --> 00:15:02.000
The trouble is that wood pulp is not very nutritious.

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:08.040
And this creature has to eat it for at least a year before it's this size, which is full

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:09.040
grown.

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:20.440
But then this larva will turn into an adult, which is equally monstrous.

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:27.600
Emerging from beneath the ground where it has lived and fed as a larva is a beetle,

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:38.120
one of the biggest in the world, the Atlas beetle.

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:43.560
Animals like this one are armed with long horns, powerful weapons with which to compete with

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:47.120
rivals for a mate.

00:15:47.120 --> 00:15:53.040
It now spends most of its time above the ground, barging its way through the undergrowth where

00:15:53.040 --> 00:16:00.720
it feeds on tree sap and fallen fruit.

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:11.680
This hefty, powerful creature may not look as if it could fly, but it can.

00:16:11.680 --> 00:16:19.320
At key moments in its life, it takes to the air to look for new sources of food and, of

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:27.320
course, a female.

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:33.760
All this burrowing and mouncing around could injure delicate flight wings.

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:41.320
So beetles have hardened the front pair to form this pair of protective covers.

00:16:41.320 --> 00:16:54.840
And the delicate flight pair are stowed away in safety underneath.

00:16:54.840 --> 00:17:11.140
To see how the wings are folded away beneath their covers, we need to wait for takeoff.

00:17:11.140 --> 00:17:38.240
As it flaps, sprung hinges click open and the wings are stretched to their full size.

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:44.440
The working wings create lift in just the same way that the dragonfly's wings do.

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:49.440
And the front wings, which have now become covers, are held out to the side.

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:52.840
And their shape does give a little extra lift.

00:17:52.840 --> 00:18:04.080
But it's clear that this is really all a clumsy fly.

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:08.640
Landings can be clumsy, too.

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:14.200
And now those fragile wings must be carefully packed away beneath their covers.

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:23.360
They're guided by a line of tiny hairs at the base of the abdomen.

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:29.720
These grip the wings and help push them into position.

00:18:29.720 --> 00:18:35.100
The beetle does it with all the care and precision that a skydiver uses when packing

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:40.760
away his parachute.

00:18:40.760 --> 00:18:46.680
Once in a new territory, it will stake out a fresh source of food and then defend it

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:51.840
until a female arrives.

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:56.000
The beetle way of life proved astonishingly successful.

00:18:56.000 --> 00:19:02.480
There are over 370,000 different species of beetle so far discovered.

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:05.560
Unbelievable figure.

00:19:05.560 --> 00:19:12.360
So early on, the beetles managed to fly as much as they need to with just one pair of

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:13.360
wings.

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:26.600
And then, around 57 million years ago, came another key development in the history of

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:27.600
flight.

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:37.200
A new type of insect appeared with two pairs of wings that became, in effect, huge billboards.

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:49.000
Those that are perhaps the most dazzlingly beautiful of all.

00:19:49.000 --> 00:20:00.440
Butterflies.

00:20:00.440 --> 00:20:05.560
Butterflies live on nectar, which they collect from flowers.

00:20:05.560 --> 00:20:10.680
Like dragonflies and beetles, they also fly to find a mate.

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:28.980
But the way they beat their colorful wings is significantly different.

00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:37.840
This lovely creature has two pairs of wings, but it has, in effect, turned them into one.

00:20:37.840 --> 00:20:43.720
It's done that quite simply by overlapping the larger front pair over the smaller hind

00:20:43.720 --> 00:20:50.600
pair so that when the front pair beat down, they automatically press down the lower pair.

00:20:50.600 --> 00:20:56.320
The lower pair themselves don't have the muscles to beat down, but just enough strength to

00:20:56.320 --> 00:21:01.080
return up.

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:06.880
Butterflies' overlapping wings, compared to the size of their bodies, are enormous, around

00:21:06.880 --> 00:21:20.160
10 times the size of other insect wings.

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:26.980
Because the wing is larger, each beat can generate a huge amount of lift.

00:21:26.980 --> 00:21:34.440
So to stay airborne, a butterfly needs to flap less often than other insects.

00:21:34.440 --> 00:21:43.240
But that slow wing beat also enables it to make rapid and unpredictable changes of direction.

00:21:43.240 --> 00:21:48.840
And that allows butterflies to fly in that zigzag, erratic way, which makes them so difficult

00:21:48.840 --> 00:22:11.520
to catch if you're a butterfly collector, or more importantly, a predator.

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:16.840
The combined front and hind wings of a butterfly not only constitute very effective flying

00:22:16.840 --> 00:22:20.480
mechanisms, they can also carry messages.

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:23.960
In fact, they carry some of the loveliest advertisements in the whole of the animal

00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:34.720
kingdom, like, for example, this beautiful golden birdwing butterfly from Borneo.

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:41.280
The butterfly's huge wings provide a spacious canvas on which to display fantastically elaborate

00:22:41.280 --> 00:22:44.400
designs.

00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:49.880
So how are these flying advertisements created?

00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:59.280
The secret lies in the microscopic structure of the wing's surface.

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:05.360
These overlapping scales, lined up like tiles on a roof, have evolved from bristles that

00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:10.960
were once tiny sensors.

00:23:10.960 --> 00:23:23.200
Some contain tiny packets of pigment that give the wings color.

00:23:23.200 --> 00:23:28.960
Others have a complex structure which splits the light so that when viewed from a particular

00:23:28.960 --> 00:23:44.880
angle, it reflects a brilliant iridescence.

00:23:44.880 --> 00:23:50.720
There are over 18,000 species of butterfly around the world, and each has wings with

00:23:50.720 --> 00:23:54.560
their own distinctive design.

00:23:54.560 --> 00:24:00.800
These ravishing colors and delectable patterns, of course, enable a male butterfly and a female

00:24:00.800 --> 00:24:05.400
butterfly to know whether or not they belong to the same species.

00:24:05.400 --> 00:24:21.160
And a mature adult, ready to mate, can identify a suitable partner from surprising distances.

00:24:21.160 --> 00:24:29.960
When a male and female eventually meet, they flutter around each other in a ritual dance.

00:24:29.960 --> 00:24:42.040
Each is checking out the flying skills and wing patterns of the other.

00:24:42.040 --> 00:24:54.560
If both pass the test, they mate.

00:24:54.560 --> 00:25:01.200
The sheer size of butterfly wings might seem to condemn their owners to a slow, almost

00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:03.720
dawdling flight.

00:25:03.720 --> 00:25:10.520
But they can be much more efficient aeronauts than you might suppose.

00:25:10.520 --> 00:25:15.960
Butterflies may not be able to fly very fast, but astonishingly, for such frail-looking

00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:23.880
creatures, they can travel for hundreds of miles in search of food.

00:25:23.880 --> 00:25:29.320
New discoveries are revealing that butterflies make immense journeys.

00:25:29.320 --> 00:25:35.880
And one of the most exciting of these studies is taking place 7,000 miles west of Borneo

00:25:35.880 --> 00:25:50.160
in Europe.

00:25:50.160 --> 00:25:56.200
I am joining a research project in the upland meadows of central Spain to look for one of

00:25:56.200 --> 00:26:01.120
the greatest of all butterfly travelers.

00:26:01.120 --> 00:26:07.240
The Painted Lady.

00:26:07.240 --> 00:26:13.520
Every spring, Painted Ladies appear in Spain in great numbers.

00:26:13.520 --> 00:26:19.640
But Spain is just a stopover.

00:26:19.640 --> 00:26:25.580
And international team of scientists are uncovering evidence of an astonishing journey right across

00:26:25.580 --> 00:26:32.520
Europe and beyond.

00:26:32.520 --> 00:26:49.120
This hugely ambitious project is the brainchild of Dr. Constante Stefanescu.

00:26:49.120 --> 00:26:55.560
Detailed records of when and where Painted Ladies appear have revealed an extraordinary

00:26:55.560 --> 00:26:56.960
mass migration.

00:26:56.960 --> 00:27:07.040
We were able to collate a huge number of observations from more than 60 different countries and

00:27:07.040 --> 00:27:10.000
maybe 35,000 records.

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:11.000
Really?

00:27:11.000 --> 00:27:15.440
And many people contributing their observations.

00:27:15.440 --> 00:27:21.560
And for the first time it was possible to understand the general pattern of migration

00:27:21.560 --> 00:27:24.240
all year round.

00:27:24.240 --> 00:27:30.000
By combining this wealth of data, the team are revealing a route map that spans incredible

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:31.000
distances.

00:27:31.000 --> 00:27:36.960
And it begins in North Africa.

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:43.700
Large numbers of Painted Ladies breed in Morocco over the winter before setting out across

00:27:43.700 --> 00:27:46.500
the Mediterranean to Europe.

00:27:46.500 --> 00:27:51.160
They then follow the spring bloom north as the plants that they and their young feed

00:27:51.160 --> 00:27:54.440
on sprout leaves and flowers.

00:27:54.440 --> 00:28:00.480
In summer, they appear in Britain and Scandinavia.

00:28:00.480 --> 00:28:06.520
But no individual butterfly lives long enough to achieve this huge journey by itself.

00:28:06.520 --> 00:28:13.320
Each step is taken by a new generation.

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:22.640
So this Painted Lady in Britain is the grandchild of a butterfly that set out from Morocco.

00:28:22.640 --> 00:28:26.960
But then in autumn, all the Painted Ladies vanish.

00:28:26.960 --> 00:28:36.200
Do they simply die out or could there be a return leg to their epic migration?

00:28:36.200 --> 00:28:41.480
Searching for an answer to this mystery has given the project its most astonishing revelation

00:28:41.480 --> 00:28:44.000
yet.

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:49.680
And it comes from a part of the team based at Rothernstead Research Institute just outside

00:28:49.680 --> 00:28:51.840
London.

00:28:51.840 --> 00:29:01.560
The key discovery emerged from a surprising source, radar.

00:29:01.560 --> 00:29:06.360
Our radar has a vertical pointing beam and it illuminates a narrow column of the sky

00:29:06.360 --> 00:29:09.040
above, like shining a powerful spotlight up into the sky.

00:29:09.040 --> 00:29:14.520
And we're able to detect individual insects as they fly through that beam.

00:29:14.520 --> 00:29:20.920
The signal is so detailed it can even help identify the species.

00:29:20.920 --> 00:29:27.600
And during the autumn disappearance, the radar picked up large numbers of Painted Ladies.

00:29:27.600 --> 00:29:34.920
They weren't dying out, they were on the move and they were flying at astonishing heights.

00:29:34.920 --> 00:29:39.200
What we found was that in fact the Painted Ladies were highly abundant at heights of

00:29:39.200 --> 00:29:44.680
3, 4, 500 metres above the ground.

00:29:44.680 --> 00:29:49.720
At this great height, they were invisible to observers down below.

00:29:49.720 --> 00:29:53.040
This explained their disappearance.

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:59.480
But the butterflies had their own very good reasons to travel at such altitudes.

00:29:59.480 --> 00:30:03.040
One of the benefits of flying at 300 or 400 metres above the ground is that the wind speeds

00:30:03.040 --> 00:30:05.560
there are much faster than they are at ground level.

00:30:05.560 --> 00:30:09.520
So the insects are able to get a lot of assistance from the wind and travel much faster than

00:30:09.520 --> 00:30:11.400
they would under their own powered flight.

00:30:11.400 --> 00:30:19.840
And we see these Painted Ladies travelling at 50 or even 70 miles an hour.

00:30:19.840 --> 00:30:25.040
As well as measuring the phenomenal speed of their flight, the radar also revealed its

00:30:25.040 --> 00:30:26.040
direction.

00:30:26.040 --> 00:30:30.280
They were heading south.

00:30:30.280 --> 00:30:34.200
So where were they going?

00:30:34.200 --> 00:30:40.040
The astonishing answer came from Constante's far-flung network of observers.

00:30:40.040 --> 00:30:44.200
And the crucial piece of data was gathered in Africa.

00:30:44.200 --> 00:30:50.520
Some expeditions in Africa in October, November have shown that there is a huge arrival of

00:30:50.520 --> 00:30:52.440
butterflies at that moment.

00:30:52.440 --> 00:31:00.200
So by the end of the summer, the newborn butterflies in Europe start to migrate way back to Europe.

00:31:00.200 --> 00:31:01.200
To Africa.

00:31:01.200 --> 00:31:02.200
Really?

00:31:02.200 --> 00:31:03.200
Yeah.

00:31:03.200 --> 00:31:11.080
A final generation riding on high altitude winds makes an immense journey of up to 3,000

00:31:11.080 --> 00:31:18.280
miles to West Africa in just a matter of days.

00:31:18.280 --> 00:31:25.920
Observers on the ground and radar in the air had found proof of an amazing migration cycle.

00:31:25.920 --> 00:31:32.440
In one year, the whole cycle is made and is the succession of these six generations moving

00:31:32.440 --> 00:31:41.920
about 5,000 kilometers in one direction and 5,000 in another direction.

00:31:41.920 --> 00:31:50.080
This migration is in fact the longest made by any insect on the planet so far discovered.

00:31:50.080 --> 00:31:57.040
Some insects face a very different challenge, not flying long distances, but flying in the

00:31:57.040 --> 00:32:05.520
dark.

00:32:05.520 --> 00:32:24.400
A light trap can attract some of the most remarkable of these nocturnal fliers.

00:32:24.400 --> 00:32:30.840
Moths.

00:32:30.840 --> 00:32:36.680
Moths probably evolved to fly at night to avoid predators.

00:32:36.680 --> 00:32:42.680
Their eyes are adapted to low light, but they also use a second highly developed sense,

00:32:42.680 --> 00:32:47.280
smell.

00:32:47.280 --> 00:32:52.080
This is a male moon moth.

00:32:52.080 --> 00:32:57.320
Moths overlap their two pairs of wings in just the same way as butterflies do.

00:32:57.320 --> 00:32:59.880
And this particular moth is very special.

00:32:59.880 --> 00:33:02.000
It has an extremely short life.

00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:05.080
It will only live for a week.

00:33:05.080 --> 00:33:07.160
It won't even feed.

00:33:07.160 --> 00:33:11.400
Its only object is to find a female.

00:33:11.400 --> 00:33:18.840
And it does that with these remarkable feather-like antennae.

00:33:18.840 --> 00:33:23.480
The female emits a particular characteristic scent.

00:33:23.480 --> 00:33:29.920
With those antennae, the male can sense it from as much as a mile away.

00:33:29.920 --> 00:33:44.360
It then takes off and flies upwind until eventually it finds the source.

00:33:44.360 --> 00:33:52.800
Moths with their combined front and rear wings are also excellent fliers.

00:33:52.800 --> 00:33:58.200
Some live longer and so need to fly to find food.

00:33:58.200 --> 00:34:03.800
This Sphinx moth's favourite food is nectar.

00:34:03.800 --> 00:34:15.800
It can even hover as it drinks.

00:34:15.800 --> 00:34:22.160
So by overlapping their two pairs of wings, butterflies and moths have become very competent

00:34:22.160 --> 00:34:23.160
fliers.

00:34:23.160 --> 00:34:29.680
But there's one group of flying insects that has changed the back pair of wings into something

00:34:29.680 --> 00:34:31.760
quite, quite different.

00:34:31.760 --> 00:34:47.160
Something that enables them to perform the most extraordinary aerial gymnastics.

00:34:47.160 --> 00:34:53.120
For the final chapter in our story of flying insects, I'm returning to London.

00:34:53.120 --> 00:34:59.800
The urban jungle and its human inhabitants provide plenty of shelter and food for a particularly

00:34:59.800 --> 00:35:05.360
adaptable and numerous kind of insect.

00:35:05.360 --> 00:35:10.120
Thank you very much.

00:35:10.120 --> 00:35:16.880
An inviting meal like this one will, I'm quite sure, very soon attract a flying diner that

00:35:16.880 --> 00:35:23.680
is one of the most remarkable of all insect aeronauts.

00:35:23.680 --> 00:35:26.980
It is, of course, a fly.

00:35:26.980 --> 00:35:32.960
This particular kind, a blowfly, occurs all over the world and its ancestors have been

00:35:32.960 --> 00:35:40.920
buzzing around for at least 250 million years.

00:35:40.920 --> 00:35:46.040
Flies are so common, we tend to dismiss them as just irritating pests.

00:35:46.040 --> 00:35:49.960
But their flying abilities are truly remarkable.

00:35:49.960 --> 00:35:58.800
Watch what happens if I try and swap this one with the menu.

00:35:58.800 --> 00:36:09.640
Slowing down the action by 40 times, we can see how astonishingly agile flies are.

00:36:09.640 --> 00:36:16.320
It makes its escape in the time it takes me to blink my eye.

00:36:16.320 --> 00:36:22.120
The ability to twist and turn at such high speeds and so evade enemies has made flies

00:36:22.120 --> 00:36:31.520
the global success that they are.

00:36:31.520 --> 00:36:37.080
They are the jet fighters of the insect world and they own their maneuverability not to

00:36:37.080 --> 00:36:43.560
the shape of their wings nor the power of their muscles but to a set of highly advanced

00:36:43.560 --> 00:36:47.360
flight sensors.

00:36:47.360 --> 00:36:56.640
A fly has its own version of a fighter pilot's instrument panel, providing constant updates

00:36:56.640 --> 00:37:05.480
on speed, altitude and direction of travel.

00:37:05.480 --> 00:37:14.080
A fly gathers this flight data through its eyes and these are among the best in the business.

00:37:14.080 --> 00:37:20.880
They can process visual information around 10 times as fast as our own eyes.

00:37:20.880 --> 00:37:27.040
But in high speed maneuvers, even a fly's eyes struggle with one crucial piece of flight

00:37:27.040 --> 00:37:29.800
data.

00:37:29.800 --> 00:37:36.160
The angle of its body in the air and the way it changes, information that a human pilot

00:37:36.160 --> 00:37:41.840
would get from an instrument based on a gyroscope.

00:37:41.840 --> 00:37:51.560
And that is essential if you're going to pull off a stunt like this one.

00:37:51.560 --> 00:37:59.720
Fortunately, flies not only have eyes to guide them.

00:37:59.720 --> 00:38:06.960
They also have a second and even more remarkable set of sensors, one that is derived from that

00:38:06.960 --> 00:38:13.200
original four wing design.

00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:20.280
A fly only has a single pair of wings.

00:38:20.280 --> 00:38:24.720
The rear pair have been converted into something else.

00:38:24.720 --> 00:38:30.440
A tiny club like appendage known as a holtier.

00:38:30.440 --> 00:38:35.840
This surprisingly sophisticated organ alerts the fly to changes in the position of its

00:38:35.840 --> 00:38:40.200
body in the air.

00:38:40.200 --> 00:38:45.960
As the fly takes off, each holtier begins to beat up and down and so fast, it immediately

00:38:45.960 --> 00:38:50.880
becomes a blur.

00:38:50.880 --> 00:38:58.480
But in slow motion, we can see that it swings back and forth like a pendulum.

00:38:58.480 --> 00:39:07.480
To understand how the holtier works, we need to track its movement in a midair roll.

00:39:07.480 --> 00:39:13.720
The weighted tip of the holtier has a kind of moving inertia so that it remains on the

00:39:13.720 --> 00:39:16.920
same swinging path as the fly banks.

00:39:16.920 --> 00:39:24.240
Now, the angle between the body and the holtier changes and the base is put under strain.

00:39:24.240 --> 00:39:32.740
This triggers sensors which register the roll.

00:39:32.740 --> 00:39:41.600
The fly can then adjust its wing beat to correct any imbalance, however extreme.

00:39:41.600 --> 00:39:47.000
New studies into a second remarkable use of the holtier signal are taking place at London's

00:39:47.000 --> 00:39:52.600
Imperial College.

00:39:52.600 --> 00:39:58.680
In the department of bioengineering, experts are studying blowflies to see if their natural

00:39:58.680 --> 00:40:08.080
flight mechanics can improve the performance of man-made flyers like this drone.

00:40:08.080 --> 00:40:14.240
Flyers are incredibly manoeuvrable and if you look at their performance, one chasing

00:40:14.240 --> 00:40:20.040
another one, it's really hardly any other animal that can match this sort of aerodynamic

00:40:20.040 --> 00:40:21.920
performance.

00:40:21.920 --> 00:40:27.360
Holger has devised an experiment to investigate an intriguing connection between a fly's

00:40:27.360 --> 00:40:37.600
holtier and its other key flight sensor, its eyes.

00:40:37.600 --> 00:40:43.840
A tiny motor simulates a series of high-speed mid-air rolls.

00:40:43.840 --> 00:40:49.400
The way the fly then reacts is recorded on a specialist camera which can replay the action

00:40:49.400 --> 00:40:54.000
in slow motion.

00:40:54.000 --> 00:40:59.560
As you can see if you look closely, the head of the fly is maintained level.

00:40:59.560 --> 00:41:07.240
The body is rotating and to maintain a level gaze, they have to counter-rotate the head.

00:41:07.240 --> 00:41:12.120
Keeping the eye's level is vital if they are to gather accurate flight information and

00:41:12.120 --> 00:41:17.880
the holtiers have been identified as the crucial sensor that makes this possible.

00:41:17.880 --> 00:41:21.200
Visual system alone would just be too slow.

00:41:21.200 --> 00:41:23.120
That's where actually the holtiers come in.

00:41:23.120 --> 00:41:30.640
The holtiers are extremely fast in terms of their responses and their immediate signals

00:41:30.640 --> 00:41:35.960
that are then sent to the neck motor system and to the flight motor system, they are the

00:41:35.960 --> 00:41:42.680
first really to compensate for any disturbances and if that has happened, the visual system

00:41:42.680 --> 00:41:49.800
is perfectly well situated to cope with the rest.

00:41:49.800 --> 00:41:55.760
So flys lost a pair of wings but gained an extraordinary new flight sensor that made

00:41:55.760 --> 00:42:06.240
them the most advanced flyers in the insect world.

00:42:06.240 --> 00:42:12.920
Flight has enabled the insects as a whole to become an astonishing global success.

00:42:12.920 --> 00:42:19.720
There are twice as many insect species than there are of all other animals put together.

00:42:19.720 --> 00:42:27.720
This is a remarkable evolutionary story that spans over 320 million years.

00:42:27.720 --> 00:42:33.160
From the first four winged creatures that emerged from the water to the armour-plated

00:42:33.160 --> 00:42:41.400
beetles which colonised land away from water, the butterflies with their huge colourful

00:42:41.400 --> 00:42:50.920
wings and the stunningly skilful aerobatic flies.

00:42:50.920 --> 00:42:53.120
But skill may not be enough.

00:42:53.120 --> 00:42:56.560
Sometimes sheer size counts.

00:42:56.560 --> 00:43:03.280
The insects had the skies themselves for around 100 million years but then a new group of

00:43:03.280 --> 00:43:09.360
animals appeared, animals that could build bigger bodies and they were to lift the techniques

00:43:09.360 --> 00:43:15.360
of flying to even greater heights.

00:43:15.360 --> 00:43:20.040
As our journey through time continues, we encounter the extraordinary pioneers of a

00:43:20.040 --> 00:43:26.600
new wave of larger flyers.

00:43:26.600 --> 00:43:32.360
Monstrous winged reptiles.

00:43:32.360 --> 00:43:40.800
Range feathered dinosaurs whose ventures into the air led to the birds.

00:43:40.800 --> 00:43:46.680
And a group of mammals that conquered the pitch black of the night.

00:43:46.680 --> 00:44:03.040
The bats.
